MCHPRS Automation Protocol
==========================

This document describes the plain-text TCP protocol spoken on the automation
port (default 25585, configurable in Config.toml as `automation_port`). It
allows external scripts to read and drive the redstone IO elements (buttons,
levers, pressure plates, lamps, trapdoors, note blocks) of running redpiler
circuits without speaking the Minecraft protocol.

1. Connection
-------------
- Transport: TCP, line-oriented UTF-8 text. Every line is terminated by '\n'.
- Maximum line length: 4096 bytes.
- On connect, the server greets the client with:

      HELLO MCHPRS AUTOMATION 2

  where 2 is the protocol version. (Version 2 adds the WATCH delivery mode;
  the version-1 `WATCH X Y Z` form is still accepted and defaults to SUB.)
- Multiple connections are supported and are fully independent.
- Closing the connection removes all watches registered by that connection.

2. Coordinates
--------------
Global Minecraft block coordinates, three signed integers separated by
spaces:

      X Y Z

Example: 128 63 2048

The server maps a position to its plot using (X >> 9, Z >> 9).

3. Requests (client -> server)
------------------------------
All opcodes are case-insensitive.

  READ X Y Z
      Read the IO element at (X, Y, Z).

  USE X Y Z
      Simulate a right-click: pulse a button or toggle a lever.

  SET X Y Z STATE
      Set the absolute state of a lever or pressure plate.
      STATE is 0 or 1.

  WATCH X Y Z [MODE]
      Register the output element at (X, Y, Z) with the given delivery mode.
      MODE is SUB (subscribe) or POLL (poll), case-insensitive. SUB means
      state changes are pushed to this connection as EVENT lines; POLL means
      nothing is pushed and the client reads the state on demand with READ.
      If MODE is omitted it defaults to SUB.
      The server maintains a watch table per connection.

  UNWATCH X Y Z
      Remove a previously registered watch.

4. Responses (server -> client)
-------------------------------
Every request receives exactly one response line.

  OK READ X Y Z CODE POWERED POWER
      READ result. CODE is the element code (see section 6), POWERED is 0 or
      1 and POWER is the 0..15 output power.

  OK USE X Y Z
      USE succeeded.

  OK SET X Y Z POWERED
      SET succeeded, POWERED is the new state.

  OK WATCH X Y Z MODE
      WATCH succeeded; the connection is now registered for (X, Y, Z) with
      the delivery mode MODE (SUB or POLL).

  OK UNWATCH X Y Z
      UNWATCH succeeded.

  WARN INACTIVE X Y Z
      No active redpiler circuit on the plot containing (X, Y, Z), or the
      plot is not loaded.

  WARN NO_IO X Y Z
      The running circuit has no IO element at (X, Y, Z).

  WARN NOT_OUTPUT X Y Z
      The element at (X, Y, Z) is an input element and cannot be watched.

  WARN FAILED X Y Z
      The operation could not be applied to the element at (X, Y, Z).

  ERR invalid request: <line>
      The opcode was not recognized; the offending line is echoed back.

  ERR malformed request: <line>
      The line could not be parsed (bad coordinates or missing arguments).

5. Events (server -> client, unsolicited)
-----------------------------------------
  EVENT X Y Z CODE POWERED POWER

Pushed to every connection that has WATCHed (X, Y, Z) in SUB mode whenever
the element's state changes. WATCHes registered in POLL mode never receive
EVENT lines; their state must be queried with READ.

6. Element codes
----------------
  0 = Button          (input)
  1 = Lever           (input)
  2 = Pressure Plate  (input)
  3 = Lamp            (output)
  4 = Trapdoor        (output)
  5 = Note Block      (output)

7. Semantics
------------
- READ works on any IO element. It is passive and never changes the circuit.
- USE pulses a button for its configured duration or toggles a lever. It has
  no effect on pressure plates, lamps, trapdoors or note blocks.
- SET only applies to levers and pressure plates. It has no effect on buttons
  or output elements.
- WATCH is only valid for output elements (lamp, trapdoor, note block).
  Watching an input element yields WARN NOT_OUTPUT.
- WATCH is only confirmed with OK WATCH after the plot verifies that a
  circuit is running and the element is an output. No EVENT is pushed before
  that confirmation.
- If the target plot is not loaded, or no redpiler circuit is active there,
  the server answers WARN INACTIVE.

8. Example session
------------------
  S: HELLO MCHPRS AUTOMATION 1
  C: READ 128 63 2048
  S: OK READ 128 63 2048 1 0 0
  C: USE 128 63 2048
  S: OK USE 128 63 2048
  C: WATCH 130 64 2048
  S: OK WATCH 130 64 2048
  S: EVENT 130 64 2048 3 1 15
  C: UNWATCH 130 64 2048
  S: OK UNWATCH 130 64 2048

9. Configuration
----------------
automation_port (Config.toml, default 25585):
    The TCP port the automation listener binds on. The bind address is fixed
    to 0.0.0.0. A configured value that is not a valid u16 falls back to
    25585. If binding fails, the automation bridge is disabled and the
    Minecraft server still runs normally.
